 The procedure \fBTcl_InvalidateStringRep\fR is used
 to mark a value's string representation invalid and to
 free any storage associated with the old string representation.
+It must only be called on an unshared value (see \fBTcl_IsShared\fR),
+normally one that is about to be modified.
+Other holders of a shared value, such as the literal table or a variable,
+may keep pointers to its string representation, and they rely on it
+staying exactly as it is: a regenerated string representation is not
+guaranteed to be identical.
+A value that has a string representation must therefore keep it unchanged
+for as long as the value is shared, even when the string could be
+regenerated from the internal representation.
 .PP
 Values usually remain one type over their life,
 but occasionally a value must be converted from one type to another.
 For example, a C program might build up a string in a value
